**Strongest hit — CRITIC-H1**, the skeptic paragraph in the Oscar section: *"The skeptic's case is worth stating properly, because it is the one a smart reader brings. A debut performance, directed by Paul Thomas Anderson, played opposite DiCaprio, Sean Penn, Regina Hall, Benicio del Toro and Teyana Taylor. The most economical explanation is that she was cast well and directed well."* That is the real objection, stated in its strongest form rather than as a decoy. Every repair I filed is an attempt to bring the rest of the draft up to that paragraph's standard.

**Highest-priority concern — CRITIC-R1** (blocker): the best disconfirming fact in the record is missing, from a source the draft already uses. She concealed her *One Battle After Another* casting from her *Testaments* castmates until a trailer outed her (*Variety*, June 11 2026) — the draft quotes that same article, in the same section whose thesis is that she establishes standing by naming everyone in the room. The other three blockers are quotation-handling defects that all break toward the thesis: a 30-word splice with no ellipsis on the thesis quote (the excised middle contains the external-validation register that would blur the Type 3/6 discriminator), a universal awards claim the draft's own comments document narrowing to make "literally true," and a single published sentence severed so its halves serve as two independent evidence points.

Two notes on what I did *not* find. I went looking for adverse professional criticism of her acting and there is none — Variety's *Testaments* review is literally headlined "Chase Infiniti Is Impeccable." The draft isn't suppressing a hostile record; it never consulted the critical record at all, which cost it its best anti-skeptic argument (the show was panned as "creatively suffocated" while she drew its only acting nomination). And I dropped a candidate blocker about the biracial-identity causal chain after reading the strongest identity-critical essay on the film, which praises the casting and performance; that finding survives only as evidentiary overreach (CRITIC-C6), not as a contested reading.
